A||N||4||ZeroFiero||minor questions on SBC||||1||
Z||000000||ZeroFiero||12-20-2001||05:17 PM||||I have a few questions about the chevy v8<BR>for the forum. I can probably source<BR>a TPI 350 from a late 80's camaro. That<BR>is a 2-bolt main, iron head engine, right?<P>How much difference does a 4-bolt main<BR>make over a 2-bolt? Can I still pull high<BR>hp off 2-bolt mains?<P>Are there any aftermarket alum. heads<BR>that fit the TPI manifold?<P>So... would I be better off stripping the<BR>TPI, manifolds, engine attachments, etc off<BR>the camaro and finding an older 4-bolt... <BR>I know where I can get a late 60's early 70's<BR>iron block free... but I'm not keen on drilling all the necessary holes for the sensors and etc. Or just better off getting<BR>the camaro enginge and dropping it in?<P>Finally, someone on the forum, (I've forgotten the name, my apologies), said<BR>their v8 had a serpentine belt so that no<BR>pulleys extended into the wheel well. Could<BR>you tell me car type/parts numbers on where<BR>you got that?<P>I'm intending to use the fiero as a weekend toy/not very practical driver.. one I can<BR>take to the strip on the weekend and not be embarrassed, but can make it there without a trailer, and drive a 2-3 hour trip without<BR>major difficulty. (minor discomfort is fine.)<BR>I don't have many strips/tracks near me.. so<BR>I have to be able to get the car there without it failing on the road.<P>TIA,<BR>Zerofiero<P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||ray b||12-20-2001||06:31 PM||||ARCHIE IS THE MAN <BR>V8ARCHIE WEB SITE<BR>SEARCH HERE ON LINE OR IN FIERO LINKS<P>4 BOLTS FOR HI POWER<BR>THEY COME WITH BETTER GUTS TOO<BR>2 BOLTS ALL HAVE JUNK RODS AND CRANKS<BR>WORTH THE HUNT AND EXTRA $$$ UP FRONT<BR>SAVE MORE IN THE LONG RUN BY BETTER STARTING POINT <P>REAL CHEAP POWER FROM CARBS<BR>WHY TB NO POWER THERE<BR><P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfixit58||12-20-2001||07:30 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by ray b:<BR><B>ARCHIE IS THE MAN <BR>V8ARCHIE WEB SITE<BR>SEARCH HERE ON LINE OR IN FIERO LINKS<P>4 BOLTS FOR HI POWER<BR>THEY COME WITH BETTER GUTS TOO<BR>2 BOLTS ALL HAVE JUNK RODS AND CRANKS<BR>WORTH THE HUNT AND EXTRA $$$ UP FRONT<BR>SAVE MORE IN THE LONG RUN BY BETTER STARTING POINT <P>REAL CHEAP POWER FROM CARBS<BR>WHY TB NO POWER THERE<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>I disagree (except with the Archie part). I've got a buddy who runs a two bolt main motor is his 69 Camaro Vintage Road racing car. He regularly spins his motor to 7,000 rpm without a problem. Unless your going to make a 1/4 drag racer, a two bolt main block is fine.<P>A TPI will make great bottom end torque but starts to drop off above 5,000. Now, how many of you are going above that on the street on a regular basis. Yes, there are better free flowing intakes for carbed or throttle body (electric carb) but you'll give up the MPFI. Or, you could spend $1,500 or more on a custom fuel injection unit but really... why?<P>My $0.02<P><BR><P>------------------<BR>Roy  :D <BR>Just another stock Blue 87 GT   <BR>Suncoast Fieros<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/pennocksize.JPG">          <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <P><B>JUST ADDED: </B>1988 GT w/ T-Tops, 5-Speed, and Performance Sound.||||reg||1
Z||000003||AnimalGT||12-21-2001||02:53 AM||||I agree with mrfixit58.  My father used to build these motors for the local short track left turners, and I've built a few HO SBCs myself.  <BR>  A 2 bolt main will holdup just as well as a 4 bolt in all but the most extreme applications.  In fact, a 2 bolt block can easily be machined to a 4 bolt, using different main caps.  Factory 4 bolts do have better internals, but people who have a 4 bolt usually want more for it than it's worth.  A very dependable high output motor can be built from a 2 bolt main motor.  <BR>  Speed costs; it's all a matter of the money/time you want to invest.<BR>  For the other info you wanted, look through a PAW, Summit, or similar catalog.  You'll find most of the things you're looking for.  To keep pulleys out of the wheel well, use an electric water pump. <P>Scott  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||ZeroFiero||12-21-2001||10:09 AM||||Thanks guys. That was most of what I was <BR>looking for.<P>Ray b: Archie is the man, and I'll buy one<BR>of his kits when I have the rest of the parts<BR>together. I'm tired of carbs, though and<BR>want to go with FI.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Mr fixit58: You're right, I virtually<BR>never need above 5K rpm on the street<BR>and I drive a low torque rotary. With<BR>an SBC, I would need far less rpm.<P>AnimalGT: Thanks, I hadn't thought<BR>of an electric water pump... should<BR>be more efficient than the mechanical<BR>one, hey?<P>As for the TPI manifold needing more flow..<BR>I had thought it could be ported/polished<BR>whatever, the same as a head. Even if<BR>not, It should have as much flow as I need,<BR>anyway. <P>ZeroFiero||||reg||14
